      "markdown": "## In plain English\n\nA char represents one UTF-16 code unit. Many familiar characters fit in one char; some symbols, such as many emoji, need more than one.\n\n## Why you use it\n\nRepresent a simple operator or letter when a whole string is unnecessary.\n\n## Small example\n\n```csharp\nchar operation = '+';\nstring text = Console.ReadLine() ?? \"\";\nif (text.Length == 1) operation = text[0];\n```\n\nExamples are small fragments; declarations may belong inside a class, and SQL/HTTP examples are shown as comments.\n\n## Common mistake\n\nReadLine returns string or null, not char. Single quotes are for char literals; double quotes are for strings.\n\n## Check your understanding\n\nWhy can char choice = Console.ReadLine(); not compile?"
