      "title": "Your professional Study App prompt",
      "group": "Roadmap reference",
      "markdown": "# Professional prompt: my C#/.NET Study App\n\nAct as a senior C#/.NET educator and a careful software engineer. Improve my existing Study App so it becomes my single learning home, taking me from my current beginner level toward an internship or junior .NET developer role.\n\nI am a student using Windows, Visual Studio, C#, GitHub and a solution named Practice.slnx. I have built small console exercises and am learning nullable input, validation, conditions, methods and loops. Explain new ideas step by step without assuming that I already understand programming terminology.\n\n## Content to incorporate\n\n1. Use my latest 40-week C#/.NET career roadmap as the main sequence. Preserve its diagnostic, first seven days, weekly deliverables, phase assessments, flagship project, resources, interview preparation, job-search process and first-job guidance. Treat the schedule as adjustable, not an employment guarantee.\n2. Incorporate my existing notes for Bro Code’s C# course at https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wxznTygnRfQ. Keep chapter timestamps and examples. Clearly distinguish chapter-based summaries and supplementary explanations from verified transcript material. Do not invent quotations or claim to have watched unavailable content.\n3. Add a beginner-friendly glossary covering data types, variables, values, functions/methods, parameters, arguments, scope, nullability, classes, objects, encapsulation, inheritance, polymorphism, interfaces, collections, exceptions, async, databases, APIs, testing and development tools.\n4. For each term, include a plain-English definition, why it matters, a small example, a common mistake, a recall question and a link to the relevant roadmap week.\n5. Explain my recurring confusions explicitly: null versus an empty string versus whitespace; string? versus ??; validation versus conversion; while loops and end-of-input; char versus string; folders versus projects versus solutions; Markdown fences versus C# source code.\n\n## Learning experience\n\nOrganize the content into clearly named sections. Let me search across lessons and definitions, see the next incomplete week, record personal notes and evidence, and mark lessons complete or reopen them. Link lessons to useful video chapters and definitions. Make code blocks, tables, links and long lessons readable on both narrow and wide screens.\n\n## Implementation safeguards\n\nInspect the app before changing it. Reuse its design, offline architecture and storage conventions. Preserve all existing algorithms, notes and progress; do not replace unrelated features or seed duplicate user notes. Include learning progress and notes in the existing backup and additive restore workflow, including compatibility with older backups. Handle storage failures honestly. Keep local data private; do not publish or upload it.\n\nVerify content completeness, search, navigation, safe content rendering, completion persistence, note saving, backup compatibility and existing regression tests. Report exactly what changed, how to open it, what was tested and any remaining limitations. Ask only for missing information that genuinely prevents safe implementation.\n"
