      "markdown": "## Networking fundamentals\n\nUnderstand how computers exchange data and why a request can fail at different layers. The Internet is the network infrastructure; the Web is one service built on it.\n\nThis is an introductory guide inferred from your course title, not your lecturer’s syllabus or an exam checklist. Compare it with your actual lectures and assignments.\n\n## Before you start\n\nBasic computer use. Learn binary place values before doing subnet exercises.\n\n## Main points explained\n\n- Layers and encapsulation: applications send data through transport, internet and link mechanisms. Each layer adds information needed for delivery; OSI is a conceptual model, not a literal inventory of every implementation.\n- Switch versus router: a switch forwards link-layer frames within a LAN; a router forwards IP packets between networks.\n- Addressing: a MAC address serves a link-layer role; an IP address identifies an interface in an IP network. A subnet prefix separates network and host portions.\n- TCP and UDP: TCP provides an ordered byte stream with reliability mechanisms. UDP provides datagrams without built-in delivery guarantees; applications may add their own reliability.\n- DNS, DHCP and ports: DNS resolves names, DHCP can supply network configuration, and transport ports help identify application endpoints.\n- Troubleshooting: distinguish link failure, addressing, routing, name resolution, transport reachability and application errors. A failed ping alone does not prove a server is down.\n\n## Step-by-step practice\n\n1. Draw the path from your browser through your router to a website; label DNS, IP and the web service.\n2. Inspect your own computer’s IP configuration and DNS lookup results. Do not change a school or shared network.\n3. Create a fault table: wrong DNS server, disconnected cable, closed service port. State which evidence distinguishes them.\n\n## Recall questions\n\n1. Does DNS assign your computer an IP address?\n2. Why does reaching an IP not prove a website works?\n3. Is a port the same as an IP address?\n\nTry answering aloud before opening the answer guidance below. Record your explanation and evidence in your personal notes.\n\n## Answer guidance\n\n1. **Does DNS assign your computer an IP address?** No. DNS resolves names; DHCP commonly supplies host network configuration.\n2. **Why does reaching an IP not prove a website works?** The application, name, TLS configuration or requested route may still fail.\n3. **Is a port the same as an IP address?** No. IP identifies a network endpoint/interface; a transport port helps select a service endpoint.\n\n## Common mistake\n\nTreating every connection problem as a DNS problem. Investigate one layer at a time using your own devices or an authorized lab.\n\n## Further study · primary resources\n\n- [MDN · How the Internet works](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Learn_web_development/Howto/Web_mechanics/How_does_the_Internet_work)\n\nThese links are supplementary reading, not your university’s prescribed reading list. External pages need internet access and may change; use current official guidance for real legal, business or security decisions.\n\n## When to mark this guide reviewed\n\n- Explain the main ideas in your own words.\n- Complete the small exercise and record its outcome.\n- Answer the recall questions and note any uncertainty.\n- Compare with your lecturer’s syllabus to identify missing material.\n\nReview status tracks this guide only. It does not mean that you have passed or mastered the entire university subject."
