      "markdown": "## Operating-system fundamentals\n\nAn operating system manages hardware resources and provides services to applications. Start with what you can observe on your computer before studying internal scheduling algorithms.\n\nThis is an introductory guide inferred from your course title, not your lecturer’s syllabus or an exam checklist. Compare it with your actual lectures and assignments.\n\n## Before you start\n\nKnow what files, folders and installed programs are.\n\n## Main points explained\n\n- Kernel and user space: the kernel performs privileged resource management; normal applications run with restricted access and request services through system calls.\n- Program versus process: a program is stored instructions; a process is a running instance with resources such as memory and handles.\n- Threads: execution paths within a process share its address space. Several threads can make coordination necessary.\n- Memory: RAM holds active data; virtual memory gives processes managed address spaces. Storage capacity is not the same thing as available RAM.\n- Files and permissions: paths locate data, while access rules decide who may read or modify it. Relative paths depend on the working directory.\n- Shell and system tools: a shell accepts commands; a GUI presents graphical controls. Process lists, logs and file metadata are evidence when diagnosing failures.\n\n## Step-by-step practice\n\n1. Run your console app and locate its process in Task Manager. Observe CPU and memory without terminating unrelated processes.\n2. Use Get-Location and Get-ChildItem to explain an absolute versus relative project path.\n3. Create a disposable folder and document file properties, ownership and access; do not change permissions on system folders.\n\n## Recall questions\n\n1. Can one program have several processes?\n2. Why can a relative path work in one terminal and fail in another?\n3. Why are kernel operations privileged?\n\nTry answering aloud before opening the answer guidance below. Record your explanation and evidence in your personal notes.\n\n## Answer guidance\n\n1. **Can one program have several processes?** Yes. Several running instances can use the same stored program.\n2. **Why can a relative path work in one terminal and fail in another?** The terminals may have different working directories.\n3. **Why are kernel operations privileged?** They control shared hardware and isolation that ordinary applications must not arbitrarily bypass.\n\n## Common mistake\n\nAssuming administrator access is the normal fix for every failure. First verify the path, operation and exact error.\n\n## Further study · primary resources\n\n- [Operating Systems: Three Easy Pieces · free textbook](https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/)\n\nThese links are supplementary reading, not your university’s prescribed reading list. External pages need internet access and may change; use current official guidance for real legal, business or security decisions.\n\n## When to mark this guide reviewed\n\n- Explain the main ideas in your own words.\n- Complete the small exercise and record its outcome.\n- Answer the recall questions and note any uncertainty.\n- Compare with your lecturer’s syllabus to identify missing material.\n\nReview status tracks this guide only. It does not mean that you have passed or mastered the entire university subject."
