      "markdown": "## Introduction to data security\n\nProtect information against inappropriate disclosure, alteration and loss. Start with the data and the threat, then choose a proportionate control.\n\nThis is an introductory guide inferred from your course title, not your lecturer’s syllabus or an exam checklist. Compare it with your actual lectures and assignments.\n\n## Before you start\n\nBasic files, networks and application input/output.\n\n## Main points explained\n\n- CIA triad: confidentiality limits disclosure, integrity protects correctness, and availability keeps authorized access possible. A control can affect more than one goal.\n- Threat, vulnerability and risk: a threat can cause harm; a vulnerability makes harm possible; risk considers consequences and likelihood in context.\n- Identity and access: authentication establishes identity; authorization limits actions. Apply least privilege and verify access at the server, not just in the UI.\n- Encryption, hashing and encoding: encryption protects data using keys; cryptographic hashing produces a digest; encoding changes representation. Base64 is not encryption.\n- Data lifecycle: collect only necessary personal data, define its purpose, control access and plan retention and deletion. Rules depend on context and applicable law.\n- Resilience and safe handling: protect credentials, keep software updated, recognize phishing, maintain recoverable backups and report incidents through the agreed process.\n\n## Step-by-step practice\n\n1. Invent a fictional student-record system and list its data, owners and permitted readers.\n2. Choose three threats and map each to a preventive control, a detection method and a recovery action.\n3. Write a safe handling checklist for your portfolio: no real student records, passwords, tokens or private logs in Git.\n\n## Recall questions\n\n1. Does Base64 protect a secret?\n2. Is login enough to protect every order?\n3. How can a backup fail confidentiality?\n\nTry answering aloud before opening the answer guidance below. Record your explanation and evidence in your personal notes.\n\n## Answer guidance\n\n1. **Does Base64 protect a secret?** No. It is reversible encoding without a secret key.\n2. **Is login enough to protect every order?** No. Each operation still needs authorization, including resource ownership checks.\n3. **How can a backup fail confidentiality?** An unprotected copy can expose the same sensitive data as the original.\n\n## Common mistake\n\nInventing cryptographic algorithms or testing attacks on other people’s systems. Use established tools and only authorized, isolated learning environments.\n\n## Further study · primary resources\n\n- [NIST · Cybersecurity Framework](https://www.nist.gov/cyberframework)\n- [UODO · principles of personal-data processing (Polish PDF)](https://uodo.gov.pl/file/4400)\n\nThese links are supplementary reading, not your university’s prescribed reading list. External pages need internet access and may change; use current official guidance for real legal, business or security decisions.\n\n## When to mark this guide reviewed\n\n- Explain the main ideas in your own words.\n- Complete the small exercise and record its outcome.\n- Answer the recall questions and note any uncertainty.\n- Compare with your lecturer’s syllabus to identify missing material.\n\nReview status tracks this guide only. It does not mean that you have passed or mastered the entire university subject."
