      "markdown": "## Information-security management\n\nManage security as an ongoing organizational responsibility. Technical controls need owners, policies, evidence, budgets and review—not just installation.\n\nThis is an introductory guide inferred from your course title, not your lecturer’s syllabus or an exam checklist. Compare it with your actual lectures and assignments.\n\n## Before you start\n\nCIA, threats, access control and data handling from introductory security.\n\n## Main points explained\n\n- Assets and ownership: identify valuable information, supporting systems and accountable people. Missing ownership makes controls hard to maintain.\n- Risk assessment: describe a threat scenario, affected assets, likelihood and impact. A numeric score is a prioritization aid, not an exact prediction.\n- Risk treatment: reduce, avoid, share or accept risk according to context; assign an owner and review date. Residual risk remains after controls.\n- Policies and procedures: policy states expected behavior; procedures explain repeatable actions. Training and evidence matter more than an unread document.\n- Incident response and continuity: prepare roles, communication, containment, recovery and lessons learned. RTO concerns restoration time; RPO concerns acceptable data loss measured in time.\n- Governance and improvement: use audits, exercises and meaningful measures to review effectiveness. NIST’s framework connects governance with identifying, protecting, detecting, responding and recovering.\n\n## Step-by-step practice\n\n1. Build a five-row risk register for a fictional university club portal: scenario, likelihood, impact, treatment, owner and review date.\n2. Write a one-page account-offboarding procedure with evidence that access was actually removed.\n3. Run a paper incident exercise for an exposed test credential; describe containment, recovery, communications and prevention.\n\n## Recall questions\n\n1. Is accepting risk the same as ignoring it?\n2. How do RTO and RPO differ?\n3. Does a policy prove compliance or security?\n\nTry answering aloud before opening the answer guidance below. Record your explanation and evidence in your personal notes.\n\n## Answer guidance\n\n1. **Is accepting risk the same as ignoring it?** No. Acceptance should be deliberate, authorized, documented and reviewed.\n2. **How do RTO and RPO differ?** RTO targets restoration time; RPO describes a tolerable recovery point or data-loss window.\n3. **Does a policy prove compliance or security?** No. You need appropriate implementation and evidence; actual obligations depend on the situation.\n\n## Common mistake\n\nClaiming certification or legal compliance from completing a checklist. Your guide is a learning exercise, not an audit.\n\n## Further study · primary resources\n\n- [NIST · Cybersecurity Framework](https://www.nist.gov/cyberframework)\n- [UODO · principles of personal-data processing (Polish PDF)](https://uodo.gov.pl/file/4400)\n\nThese links are supplementary reading, not your university’s prescribed reading list. External pages need internet access and may change; use current official guidance for real legal, business or security decisions.\n\n## When to mark this guide reviewed\n\n- Explain the main ideas in your own words.\n- Complete the small exercise and record its outcome.\n- Answer the recall questions and note any uncertainty.\n- Compare with your lecturer’s syllabus to identify missing material.\n\nReview status tracks this guide only. It does not mean that you have passed or mastered the entire university subject."
