      "markdown": "## Internet of Things (IoT)\n\nConnect physical observations or actions to software. An IoT system joins a device, communication path and application, each with its own failure modes.\n\nThis is an introductory guide inferred from your course title, not your lecturer’s syllabus or an exam checklist. Compare it with your actual lectures and assignments.\n\n## Before you start\n\nProgramming and basic networking. Use a simulator or instructor hardware; buying a kit is not required for this guide.\n\n## Main points explained\n\n- Sensors and actuators: a sensor measures something; an actuator changes something. Sampling, calibration and units determine what measurements mean.\n- Microcontrollers and computers: constrained devices have limited memory, power and processing. Timing and hardware interfaces matter alongside code.\n- Digital and analog signals: pins, converters and communication buses connect components. Respect the specific board’s voltage and current limits.\n- Telemetry and commands: telemetry reports observations; commands request actions. HTTP and publish/subscribe messaging support different communication patterns.\n- Reliability: timestamps, retries, duplicate messages, missing readings and offline buffering need explicit behavior. Do not treat a missing reading as a valid zero.\n- Device security: protect identity, credentials, updates and network access. A physically accessible device and its backend both belong in the threat model.\n\n## Step-by-step practice\n\n1. Simulate a temperature sensor producing timestamped fictional readings; include one missing or invalid reading.\n2. Send the readings to a local test API or save them in a file, then display a summary with units.\n3. Simulate disconnection and duplicate delivery. Define what is retried, discarded or flagged without controlling real equipment.\n\n## Recall questions\n\n1. Is a temperature sensor an actuator?\n2. Why attach units and timestamps?\n3. Why can retrying cause duplicates?\n\nTry answering aloud before opening the answer guidance below. Record your explanation and evidence in your personal notes.\n\n## Answer guidance\n\n1. **Is a temperature sensor an actuator?** No. It measures; a heater or motor changes the environment.\n2. **Why attach units and timestamps?** Values need physical meaning and a time context to be interpreted correctly.\n3. **Why can retrying cause duplicates?** The receiver may have accepted a message even when the sender did not receive its acknowledgement.\n\n## Common mistake\n\nConnecting unknown voltages or experimenting with mains electricity. Use only safe instructor-approved low-voltage setups or simulations.\n\n## Further study · primary resources\n\n- [Arduino · learning documentation](https://docs.arduino.cc/learn/)\n\nThese links are supplementary reading, not your university’s prescribed reading list. External pages need internet access and may change; use current official guidance for real legal, business or security decisions.\n\n## When to mark this guide reviewed\n\n- Explain the main ideas in your own words.\n- Complete the small exercise and record its outcome.\n- Answer the recall questions and note any uncertainty.\n- Compare with your lecturer’s syllabus to identify missing material.\n\nReview status tracks this guide only. It does not mean that you have passed or mastered the entire university subject."
