      "markdown": "## Data analysis and machine learning\n\nTurn data into defensible findings or predictive models. Data analysis explains observations; machine learning fits patterns that must be evaluated on appropriate unseen data.\n\nThis is an introductory guide inferred from your course title, not your lecturer’s syllabus or an exam checklist. Compare it with your actual lectures and assignments.\n\n## Before you start\n\nProgramming, tables, descriptive statistics, basic probability and algebra. Use the language and tools selected by your course.\n\n## Main points explained\n\n- Question and data quality: define what you want to learn, what a row represents and where data came from. Inspect missing values, duplicates, units and implausible ranges.\n- Exploratory analysis: use distributions, summary statistics and plots to understand variation. Correlation alone does not establish causation.\n- Features and labels: features are model inputs; labels are targets in supervised learning. Avoid information unavailable when the prediction will actually be made.\n- Task and baseline: regression predicts numeric quantities; classification predicts categories; clustering groups examples. Compare a model against a simple baseline.\n- Splits and leakage: separate training, validation and test roles. Fit preprocessing on training data; use time-aware or group-aware splits when random splitting would leak information.\n- Evaluation and generalization: choose metrics suited to the cost of errors. Check overfitting, class imbalance, subgroup performance and changing data after deployment.\n\n## Step-by-step practice\n\n1. Use a small public teaching dataset or fictional data; define one question and create a data dictionary.\n2. Inspect missingness and distributions, then write down a simple baseline before fitting anything more complex.\n3. Compare results on held-out data with an appropriate metric. Document limitations, potential leakage and one decision you cannot justify from the data.\n\n## Recall questions\n\n1. Why not evaluate only on training data?\n2. Why can accuracy mislead with rare positives?\n3. When should a scaler be fitted?\n\nTry answering aloud before opening the answer guidance below. Record your explanation and evidence in your personal notes.\n\n## Answer guidance\n\n1. **Why not evaluate only on training data?** The model may memorize or overfit examples rather than generalize.\n2. **Why can accuracy mislead with rare positives?** Predicting the majority class can appear accurate while missing nearly every important positive case.\n3. **When should a scaler be fitted?** On training data only, then applied consistently to validation/test data without learning from them.\n\n## Common mistake\n\nTrying many models against the test set until one looks good. That turns the test set into a tuning resource and weakens the final evaluation.\n\n## Further study · primary resources\n\n- [Google · Machine Learning Crash Course](https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/)\n\nThese links are supplementary reading, not your university’s prescribed reading list. External pages need internet access and may change; use current official guidance for real legal, business or security decisions.\n\n## When to mark this guide reviewed\n\n- Explain the main ideas in your own words.\n- Complete the small exercise and record its outcome.\n- Answer the recall questions and note any uncertainty.\n- Compare with your lecturer’s syllabus to identify missing material.\n\nReview status tracks this guide only. It does not mean that you have passed or mastered the entire university subject."
