      "markdown": "## Web applications\n\nBuild an application with a browser-facing interface and, when needed, a backend. For your career path, connect a small interface to ASP.NET Core rather than learning many frameworks at once.\n\nThis is an introductory guide inferred from your course title, not your lecturer’s syllabus or an exam checklist. Compare it with your actual lectures and assignments.\n\n## Before you start\n\nProgramming basics and how a browser reaches a server.\n\n## Main points explained\n\n- HTML, CSS and JavaScript: HTML provides semantic structure, CSS presentation and layout, and JavaScript interactive behavior. These responsibilities complement each other.\n- Client and server: browser code is visible and controlled by the user; trusted authorization and business rules belong on the server.\n- HTTP contracts: method, route, headers, body and status codes describe requests and responses. JSON is a data format, not a database or transport protocol.\n- Forms and validation: label inputs and describe errors accessibly. Client checks improve usability, but the server must validate independently.\n- State and identity: cookies, sessions and tokens have different roles. Consider expiration, logout, secure handling and protection against unwanted state changes.\n- Quality: responsive layouts, keyboard interaction, bounded queries, error states and tests make a demo usable. Loading and empty states need deliberate messages.\n\n## Step-by-step practice\n\n1. Build an accessible order-entry form with labeled fields and a keyboard-usable submit action.\n2. Connect it to a local API with fictional data; show both successful creation and a rejected request.\n3. Test blank fields, invalid quantities, a server failure and attempted access to another fictional user’s order.\n\n## Recall questions\n\n1. Why repeat validation on the server?\n2. Is JSON responsible for authorization?\n3. What makes an error message useful?\n\nTry answering aloud before opening the answer guidance below. Record your explanation and evidence in your personal notes.\n\n## Answer guidance\n\n1. **Why repeat validation on the server?** A caller can bypass or modify the browser interface.\n2. **Is JSON responsible for authorization?** No. It represents data; server-side rules determine allowed operations.\n3. **What makes an error message useful?** It identifies the problem, relates it to the affected input or action, and suggests a safe next step.\n\n## Common mistake\n\nTreating a hidden admin button as security. Enforce the rule at the endpoint and test with different users.\n\n## Further study · primary resources\n\n- [MDN · Learn web development](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Learn_web_development)\n- [W3C WAI · accessibility tutorials](https://www.w3.org/WAI/tutorials/)\n\nThese links are supplementary reading, not your university’s prescribed reading list. External pages need internet access and may change; use current official guidance for real legal, business or security decisions.\n\n## When to mark this guide reviewed\n\n- Explain the main ideas in your own words.\n- Complete the small exercise and record its outcome.\n- Answer the recall questions and note any uncertainty.\n- Compare with your lecturer’s syllabus to identify missing material.\n\nReview status tracks this guide only. It does not mean that you have passed or mastered the entire university subject."
