      "markdown": "## Software engineering\n\nDeliver software that solves a real requirement and can be maintained by other people. Writing code is one activity within clarification, design, testing, review and operation.\n\nThis is an introductory guide inferred from your course title, not your lecturer’s syllabus or an exam checklist. Compare it with your actual lectures and assignments.\n\n## Before you start\n\nAbility to implement and debug a small program; introductory Git.\n\n## Main points explained\n\n- Requirements: separate functional behavior from qualities such as reliability and performance. Make ambiguous words like “fast” or “secure” testable in context.\n- Acceptance criteria: describe observable conditions for completion, including failure cases. A user story is a starting conversation, not a complete specification.\n- Design and modularity: aim for focused responsibilities and clear interfaces. Abstraction should reduce real complexity rather than add layers without a purpose.\n- Version control and review: small meaningful commits, branches and explained pull requests make changes easier to inspect and recover.\n- Testing and CI: combine useful unit and integration tests with automated builds. Tests reduce risk but cannot prove absence of every defect.\n- Lifecycle and maintenance: plan, implement, evaluate and adapt. Track defects, dependencies, operational behavior and technical debt after the initial release.\n\n## Step-by-step practice\n\n1. Write a user story and five acceptance criteria for cancelling an order, including forbidden state transitions.\n2. Implement a small change with tests and a focused commit; explain what changed and what you deliberately excluded.\n3. Ask a peer to review the diff or do a structured self-review, then update documentation and record remaining tradeoffs.\n\n## Recall questions\n\n1. What is a non-functional requirement?\n2. Why keep a pull request small?\n3. Is refactoring the same as adding a feature?\n\nTry answering aloud before opening the answer guidance below. Record your explanation and evidence in your personal notes.\n\n## Answer guidance\n\n1. **What is a non-functional requirement?** A required quality or constraint, such as a specified response-time target or accessibility expectation.\n2. **Why keep a pull request small?** Reviewers can understand its purpose, verify behavior and spot errors more effectively.\n3. **Is refactoring the same as adding a feature?** No. Refactoring changes internal structure while aiming to preserve observable behavior.\n\n## Common mistake\n\nChoosing elaborate architecture before understanding the requirement. Start with a clear, testable vertical feature.\n\n## Further study · primary resources\n\n- [Microsoft Learn · C# documentation](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/tour-of-csharp/)\n\nThese links are supplementary reading, not your university’s prescribed reading list. External pages need internet access and may change; use current official guidance for real legal, business or security decisions.\n\n## When to mark this guide reviewed\n\n- Explain the main ideas in your own words.\n- Complete the small exercise and record its outcome.\n- Answer the recall questions and note any uncertainty.\n- Compare with your lecturer’s syllabus to identify missing material.\n\nReview status tracks this guide only. It does not mean that you have passed or mastered the entire university subject."
